# Authoring workflow: September 7 comparison and unresolved questions

## September 14 prospective sample calibration

Jörn reviewed the fixed two-page Astra data-science sample (PDF SHA-256
`01d63be3d72999230e98c0b5fb6f89dcba33d532188d83894f4ad01a9f62c58c`)
and found the prose comfortable and its writing quality good/passing, while
finding the narrative too chaotic and insufficiently structured. His exact
feedback and revision history are preserved at
[`docs/review-evidence/human-feedback/ds-reading-2026-09-14.md`](../docs/review-evidence/human-feedback/ds-reading-2026-09-14.md).
The direct message and both later coordinator relays originate in root thread
`01a0a021-b283-7fa3-9e16-aec87ce950df`.
This is specific prose feedback, not full-chapter or thesis PASS.

The chapter's intended story is: sample varied polytopes and geometric
measurements; temporarily set semantics aside to find statistical patterns;
interpret those patterns geometrically; formulate conjectures and seek proofs
that yield mathematical insight. Method and sampling details must serve that
story. Distinguish this intended route from what the experiments achieved.

Jörn further clarified to the DS agent that this originating context must
guide research prioritization, not only prose. He permits both this discovery
frame and a search/allocation frame, or a better superseding approach when
supported by evidence. This does not invalidate the recent experiments.
The DS agent acknowledged that its recent operational frame had been mainly
high-ratio search/allocation, without establishing that it superseded the
original mathematical-discovery aim. The recovered feedback record preserves
that correction's practical consequence: current DS triage must assess both the
discovery route and high-ratio search against the evidence actually retained.
Existing ridge-area/alignment anatomy and a product-width derivation were
identified as semantic-reconstruction leads; whether either earns space in the
assembled chapter remains a current editorial/scientific decision.

The coordinator froze an Astra reading review and a Luna layout review before
the feedback; ordinary copies are under
[`docs/review-evidence/calibration/`](../docs/review-evidence/calibration/).
Astra predicted reduced decoding burden and some local placement issues but
missed the major narrative objection. The
[`DS sample comparison`](../docs/review-evidence/calibration/ds-sample-comparison.md)
records that miss without retroactively improving predictions. There is no
validated substitute for human narrative review. Checking section sequence
against the research purpose is a retained review hypothesis, not a proven gate.

On the revised ten-page chapter's opening, Jörn found the first sentence
confusing and objected to unnecessary example lists, specifically “A strong
ordering, a successful prediction or a small collection of informative
features”: no special attention to strong ordering was needed there. He also
queried “our approach was” versus “is”, without prescribing a tense. This is
additional negative prose evidence despite the earlier sample's comfortable
prose. The DS author owns the revision. The current repair hypothesis is to
state the approach directly and omit enumerations that add no needed
distinction; it is not a ban on mathematically necessary lists or a validated
general writing rule.

On the next inline revision, Jörn explicitly liked the new first sentence,
“We use data science to look for geometric relationships involving symplectic
capacity.” He then stopped at “compute quantities” to ask which quantities
and whether selection was broad/opportunistic. He requested joining the
clarification with a comma so it is read before that pause. This is concrete
sequential-reading evidence: splitting sentences can introduce an avoidable
question, not only reduce reading effort. It approves the first sentence,
not the full paragraph or chapter. The preserved feedback record linked above
gives the exact wording and revision history. The useful cross-case lesson is
about supplying information before the reader needs to stop and ask for it; it
is not a universal preference for long sentences.

## September 14 HKO reading

The fixed seven-page HKO PDF and its SHA-256 are identified in the preserved
[`HKO reading feedback`](../docs/review-evidence/calibration/hko-reading-feedback.md).
The direct comments originate in root thread
`01a0a021-b283-7fa3-9e16-aec87ce950df`. Jörn reported that
the computer-algebra attribution was the first point worth annotating and
praised the reading so far, then continued. Subsequent annotations identify
premature witness identifiers, missing motivation for the worked example,
unnecessary negative alternatives, a skipped inference about leaving product
deformations, and coordinates introduced before their geometric meaning.
Those comments have candidate-only revisions; the served PDF is unchanged.
Jörn subsequently finished that fixed draft and judged it already PASSing.
This is HKO approval, not whole-thesis approval.

The preserved
[`HKO reading prediction`](../docs/review-evidence/calibration/hko-reading-prediction.md)
was written before these comments arrived but after the PDF was opened. It is
not blind: the coordinator helped author the text. It identified the
witness-reference issue, but did not identify the other specific annotations.
Predicted issues the reader has not addressed remain unadjudicated. This
provides a second text/review comparison, not a validated replacement for human
exposition review.

## September 14 preliminaries readiness failure

After the abstract/introduction corrections, Jörn immediately rejected the
preliminaries opener as sloppy, then the sentence ending “without further
comment”, and skipped to Chapter 4 because of the error rate. The preceding
agent preflight had been explicitly limited to mathematical prerequisites and
inference gaps, with a style inventory excluded. Calling that enough to send
him onward confused a narrow check with prose readiness. These direct comments
also originate in root thread `01a0a021-b283-7fa3-9e16-aec87ce950df`.

The active review prompt was changed, not merely an apology recorded: a
sequential prose pass now asks about sentence purpose, clear referents,
unnecessary abstraction/detail, and intended mathematical audience. Prior human
annotations are examples, not a closed checklist. Its new findings include an
imprecise EHZ description and compressed dual-variation explanation. Parent
integration still checks mathematical meaning: one suggested QP rewrite called
the normal a velocity, and one nonsmooth suggestion used an unjustified ordinary
derivative notation. Those suggestions were corrected before integration.
Forward evidence is recorded in
[`docs/review-evidence/calibration/`](../docs/review-evidence/calibration/);
effectiveness as a substitute for Jörn's review is not established. Future work
should not label prose ready merely because an earlier mathematical preflight
passed.

## September 16 rotated-pentagon reading

The replacement chapter became a direct separation test between mathematical
and expository review. Jörn rejected an implementation-led opening, unnecessary
new terminology and vague transitions, and stopped reading when the exposition
fell below his worthwhile-review threshold. He later read the proof and accepted
the argument while still rejecting its writeup. The subject-local record at
[`docs/pentagon-chapter/user-reading-feedback.md`](../docs/pentagon-chapter/user-reading-feedback.md)
preserves the concrete triggers, response and artifact caveat; the messages
originate in root thread `01a0a021-b283-7fa3-9e16-aec87ce950df`.

The resulting decision is to preserve the analytic argument, replace the poor
presentation, and keep the ridge–capacity relation out of that chapter. This
case shows why proof acceptance cannot stand in for prose acceptance and why a
review should inspect what the reader is asked to understand before treating a
mathematically correct passage as ready. It does not establish a closed style
checklist or human approval of the completed `docs/pentagon-chapter-v2/` rewrite.

The earlier sections below retain prior evidence and unresolved comparisons.

The authoring workflow is still being discovered. Jörn's September 2026
discussion makes his full-PDF review the costly final gate, ideally passed on
the first candidate; it does not supply a trusted automated substitute. He
distinguished adaptable feedback during writing from that gate. Detailed
summary infrastructure is deferred until writing and feedback reveal its needs
([decision context](planning-context.md)).

## Knowledge that changes the approach

`docs/project-facts.md` items 40–44 record Jörn's experience: default model prose
is often overconfident, nonsensical or excessively dense, and producing useful
phrasing and deciding what belongs where are much harder than a successful
LaTeX build. Those are attributed observations, not a measurement of today's
model. Legacy prose needs revalidation under changed claims or structure even
if previously approved.

In the live discussion Jörn singled out proof jumps a human reader cannot
fill. The useful distinction is between an invalid intended bridge and a valid
bridge whose exposition needs intermediate steps. Proving the destination by
an unrelated route does not vindicate the written transition. His willingness
to investigate those distinctions is not an offer to repeatedly review whole
drafts during authoring.

One concrete prior preference is recorded in `docs/project-facts.md` item 85:
for the product-QP six-facet proof, explain why the proof is product-specific,
state theorem and algorithm before their proof, and put scope/use caveats after
the main statements. Item 9 also rejects forcing the thesis's different
research questions into one tight narrative. These are more useful starting
points than inventing a universal style checklist.

## Case used for the first authoring comparison

Inspected 2026-09-07 at `921f3c02efefbfb3919fb2c35545686459123fff`:

- `thesis/07-hko-local-maximum-exact-certificate.tex`, paragraph beginning
  “The finite first-order criterion”: positive relation and row rank imply a
  uniform strict decrease on the transverse slice.
- `thesis/07-hko-local-maximum.tex`, theorem
  `thm:hko-ten-facet-local-maximum` and “Proof spine”: the result the reader
  needs that step to establish.
- `thesis/07-hko-local-maximum-chart-reduction.tex`, final slice construction:
  how the slice conclusion returns to the geometric neighborhood and equality
  set.
- `memories/hko-author-context.md`: already-inspected support route and the
  distinction between feasible upper functions and optimizing branches.

This case was selected for workflow development, not because a mathematical
error had been identified. It is small, central to the main theorem and tests the kind of
reader bridge Jörn described. It needs no new experiment, witness search or
full verifier run. The theorem's established status is not in question.

For a reviewer checking this particular implication, the intended reasoning is:
rank and annihilation make the restricted rows span the slice dual; if every
row evaluated nonnegatively on a nonzero direction, the strictly positive zero
relation would force all evaluations to vanish, contradicting spanning.
The finite minimum of these linear evaluations is continuous and negative on
the unit sphere, hence bounded above by a negative constant. The finitely many
smooth upper functions have a uniform first-order remainder small compared
with that margin. Their touching inequalities give strict decrease of the true
ratio. This is the inspection agent's reconstruction, not an independent audit
of the upper-function hypotheses or the surrounding chart proof.

## Reader observation, alternatives and selected revision

The exact compared candidates and rendered inputs recovered from the former
authoring-trial branch are preserved in the
[`HKO authoring trial evidence`](../docs/review-evidence/calibration/hko-authoring-trial-2026-09-07/README.md).
That packet supports inspection of this case; it is frozen calibration evidence,
not a maintained workflow or an approved thesis source.

On 2026-09-07 a no-history subagent (`sprint_authoring/hko_reader`) read only
the first two TeX files above, without this memory or its supplied reconstruction.
It recovered the implication and identified two fillable but compressed steps:
the all-nonnegative contradiction from the positive relation, and the uniform
neighborhood obtained from the finite minimum on the unit sphere and a common
Taylor remainder. Its reconstruction agreed with the one above. It required
finite-dimensional linear algebra, compactness and differentiability, not
specialized nonsmooth analysis. This is one agent-reader observation, not
evidence that Jörn or the intended human reader would find the passage adequate.

The first reader proposed an expansion: explicitly exclude all-nonnegative row
evaluations; define `m(h) = min_sigma r_sigma(h)` on the unit sphere and give
`m(h) <= -c`; then state a simultaneous remainder bound
`|U_sigma(a_0+s)-U_sigma(a_0)-r_sigma(s)| <= (c/2)||s||`. This provides a concrete
authoring candidate without changing the theorem or rerunning its certificate.

Two no-history authors then proposed alternatives. Variant A ended with an
explicit linear decrease bound using a direction-dependent row; B used the
minimum over the upper functions and a uniform `-ct+o(t)` estimate. Separate
math-meaning and reader-flow reviewers inspected the original and candidate
arguments. The mathematical reviewer found both correct under the existing
smooth touching-upper-function hypotheses and preferred A's explicit bound.
The flow reviewer preferred the original plus the missing sign argument:
the constants and remainder notation in A/B expanded a routine step more than
the reader needed. This disagreement is useful evidence about the tradeoff,
not a correctness vote.

The integrated edit therefore adds only the sign argument after the spanning
sentence. It fixes the shared concrete omission without introducing new
notation. The original compactness/Taylor sentence remains. That selection is
the integrating agent's judgment; whether the intended human reader needs the
more explicit uniformity argument remains untested. Child task names for this
comparison were `sprint_authoring/variant_a`, `variant_b`, `meaning_review`, and
`flow_review`. No build or certificate run was part of the comparison.

Whether the uniformity sentence remains too compressed was left unresolved.
Jörn subsequently rejected further opportunistic passage checks as the next
priority. This trial is reference for workflow discovery, not a queued task;
see [current planning context](planning-context.md#writing-and-review).

This comparison retained a revision and reviewer observations; it did not
establish a repeatable authoring method. Jörn clarified on September 11 that
roughly 99% of the proposed experiment's value is learning how to produce
improvements at low cost; retaining its particular chapter is incidental.
A successful local attempt alone would not establish transfer to other
chapters or whole-thesis coherence.

The comparison above is one source-level authoring attempt, not a human-reader
before/after test or evidence that a whole-thesis workflow succeeds. Its
mathematical and reviewer observations remain reference material; it is not
a queued experiment.
